paragraph:
In Section 4.1, you will use Math in the World to investigate how to enlarge and reduce figures so that they maintain their shape. Your work with Geometric Relationships, such as scale drawings of maps, will lay the foundation for much of this chapter.

Maps and scale models have one thing in common: they represent Proportional Relationships. In Section 4.2, you will explore these relationships and make Connections Across Representations using tables, graphs, rules, and the Giant One.

In Section 4.3, you will continue to develop your understanding of Knowns and Unknowns by building expressions using a new tool called "algebra tiles." You will use a variable to write expressions and help you describe the perimeter and area of shapes built with tiles when one dimension is unknown or represents various possible lengths.

In this chapter, you will learn how to:
• determine solutions to problems involving proportional relationships;
• identify proportional relationships in tables, graphs, and equations;
• calculate unit rates;
• combine like terms and rewrite algebraic expressions;
• rewrite expressions by using the Distributive Property; and
• compare two algebraic expressions.

How often do you need to compare one thing to another? You probably compare prices when you are buying something, and you may compare the heights of basketball players as you watch a game. In mathematics, comparing one thing to another is an important strategy to learn about how they are related.

In Section 5.1, you will use your knowledge of Proportional Relationships, percents in particular, to solve more complicated problems involving discounts and sales, learn strategies to understand how quantities are related, and make sense of Math in the World such as with nutrition labels.

In Section 5.2, you will continue your study of the major conceptual idea, Probability and Data. You will learn how to calculate probabilities of two or more events and how to decide when different games are fair. You will also calculate the probability that more than one event will take place, using simulations and collected data. You will deepen your understanding by designing your own simulations and collecting data in order to compare experimental and theoretical probabilities. As you do this, you will learn new ways to make Connections Across Representations as you organize the possible outcomes and explore situations where one outcome is more likely than another.

In Section 5.3, you will expand on Knowns and Unknowns with the use of variables to represent a single unknown number in different contexts, such as the length of the side of a figure or the number of frogs in a pond. You will learn about the 5-D Process, a strategy to organize your thinking that can help you solve problems. You will also learn how a variable can be useful to write expressions and equations to represent situations.

In this chapter, you will:
• determine and use percents to solve problems;
• calculate the probability of compound (multiple) events;
• use simulation results to make and test conjectures about various situations;
• describe how the relationship between experimental and theoretical probabilities for an event changes as the experiment is conducted many times;
• solve real-world mathematical problems using different strategies, such as diagrams and the 5-D Process; and
• use variables to represent quantities in different situations.

When traveling in a car, have you ever asked, “When are we going to get there?” Did you know that you could use mathematics to answer that question? In Section 7.1, you will use diagrams, like the ones you used for percents, to discover the relationship between rate, time, and distance.
Then, you will revisit scale factors and connect them to percents. You will learn how to break down difficult problems by rewriting equations to remove fractional and decimal coefficients. Finally, you will investigate how to calculate the percent increase, percent decrease, and simple interest.
You have studied many different proportional relationships, including scale drawings, unit pricing, penny towers, and gas mileage. You will explore more about Proportional Relationships in Section 7.2 as you learn additional strategies for solving them.
In this chapter, you will learn how to:
• solve problems involving distance, rate, and time;
• solve equations that have fractional or decimal coefficients;
• determine the whole amount if you only know a percentage of it, and vice versa;
• calculate simple interest; and
• set up and solve proportional equations.

In this chapter, you will learn how to:
• describe, analyze, and compare sets of data using measures of central tendency, such as mean and median, and using measures of variation, including mean absolute deviation (MAD) and interquartile range (IQR);
• identify random and representative samples to complete a survey;
• identify angles by their characteristics and use correct vocabulary to describe and name them; and
• construct triangles and quadrilaterals with given side lengths and/or angles and predict whether they will be unique shapes.
